+# Shared Linux collector and activity publishing
+
+This feature changes the Linux collector, not ActivityKit authorization. A foreground iPhone must create an existing live activity, obtain an activity-scoped lease from the relay through App Attest, and send that lease over host-key-verified SSH. The collector never receives an Apple provider key, installation management credential, or global password. It does not request notification permission, send alerts, start activities remotely, or keep iOS SSH alive indefinitely.
+
+## Collection and process boundaries
+
+The no-argument executable is a stdin/stdout bridge. A detached daemon owns one live `Monitor` and one incremental historical parser pipeline for each effective Unix UID and full executable SHA-256 pair. Byte-identical executables share a daemon even when launched from different paths; different digests run independently. Each digest has its own scanning, parsing, memory and traffic baselines, so concurrent builds cost additional resources. The shared history analyzer projects requested calendar views without reparsing logs for each subscriber. Different SSH keys or digests for the same UID are not separate security tenants.
+
+The runtime directory is the canonical passwd home plus `.agent-speed/daemons/<sha256>`, not an SSH-supplied `HOME` or XDG runtime path. Home ownership and writable permissions are checked. Every runtime directory component must be owned, nonsymlink and mode 0700; `collector.lock` must be an owned, single-link regular file with mode 0600; `collector.sock` must be an owned socket with mode 0600. Both socket peers must have the same effective UID through `SO_PEERCRED`. Bind and connect use `/proc/self/fd/<held-directory-fd>/collector.sock`, avoiding Unix socket path-length limits without truncating the digest.
+
+A stable `flock` is held for the daemon's entire lifetime. The lock file is never unlinked. Concurrent bridges wait for readiness and perform a private protocol/executable SHA-256 handshake before sending commands. Only the exclusive lifecycle-lock owner may recheck and remove a refused, verified stale endpoint. Connection failures do not authorize removing a socket or killing a process. The daemon uses no TCP listener and does not depend on PID files or PID-based killing.
+
+Each subscription has its own input buffer, named timezone, history generation and bounded nonblocking output. A subscriber compares the view's projection generation, not its text, so an unchanged projection is never re-sent and a changed one is always delivered whole; the worker advances that generation only when the newly serialized projection differs from the cached one. Unsent snapshots may coalesce. History and completion frames do not silently coalesce or disappear: a subscriber exceeding the output bound is disconnected and receives a nonzero interrupted-stream failure. Consumers must treat interruption as an incomplete stream and reconnect. A destructive completion drain occurs once per sampler tick and is fanned out to connected subscribers.
+
+The live monitor retains a rolling 48-hour input window so changing a viewer's day boundary does not rebuild discovery, parsing, performance or traffic state. Calendar totals use each view's day start, including offset transitions. History projections are per view; live performance baselines retain the existing host-local-calendar meaning and do not change with an SSH viewer's timezone. Physical traffic sampling and its baseline remain shared; viewing it does not resample the machine. Unknown rates, partial histories and offline gaps retain the existing v1 semantics.
+
+Any connected subscriber or live/pending authorized lease keeps the daemon active. Closing one bridge's stdin removes only that subscriber. A confirmed lease survives the last SSH exit until expiry, explicit unsubscribe, replacement, terminal relay rejection or bounded invalid-snapshot failure. With no subscribers or leases the daemon exits after 30 seconds. Candidate leases awaiting their first successful publish expire after 75 seconds even when the SSH channel remains open; disconnecting the last waiter cancels an unconfirmed candidate.
+
+Detachment closes inherited SSH descriptors, redirects stdio to `/dev/null`, uses a readiness pipe, and ignores SIGHUP. This is not a guarantee against systemd `KillUserProcesses`, session cgroup destruction, container termination, host shutdown or account policy. Supported background operation requires that policy to allow detached same-user processes to survive the SSH session. No root service or linger setting is installed. A host policy that kills those processes requires operator action outside this feature; re-open SSH and inspect relay freshness rather than reporting continued collection.
+
+## Runtime identity and upgrades
+
+`agent-speed-collector --identity` prints exactly `shared-collector-v2 ` followed by the executable's full SHA-256 and a newline. It does not start a daemon. The no-argument bridge derives its namespace from those same bytes; callers do not pass a digest or namespace. It reuses only that digest's daemon, or starts one while holding that digest's lifecycle lock. The exact digest handshake remains mandatory: a mismatching daemon at the selected endpoint is an error, never a reason to attach, kill or fall back. Other digests, legacy global endpoints and their locks are neither contacted nor removed. Each daemon exits according to its own subscribers and authorizations; upgrading a client does not require stopping other versions. There is no forced rolling upgrade or `--stop` command.
+
+Maintained native deployment computes the bundled bytes' digest, uploads a private temporary file, verifies SHA-256, installs without replacing a conflicting file, and verifies `--identity`. It launches `~/.cache/agent-speed/collector-v2-<sha256>` without arguments. It does not overwrite the legacy `~/.cache/agent-speed/collector` path. Unsupported architecture, missing shared libraries, unsafe permissions and identity conflicts remain errors; there is no old-binary or demonstration-data fallback.
+
+Already shipped applications do not acquire this namespace behavior remotely. Their global daemon may coexist with new digest-scoped daemons; this implementation does not replace their binaries, endpoints or cache. New clients use the same digest-addressed deployment and stdio/control contracts. Each digest's history cache is isolated at the canonical passwd home's `.cache/agentspeed-collector/<sha256>/history.sqlite3`, with its own `history.lock`, WAL and SHM files; old caches are not imported or reset. Unsafe or unavailable optional caches retain the existing in-memory behavior. Source logs remain read-only shared inputs. Activity and Widget lease identifiers and relay authorization are not digest-namespaced: do not publish the same authorization from two versions. Lease owners must use the originating digest's verified bridge for explicit unsubscribe and independently revoke or replace relay authorization. Missing old binaries or failed unsubscribe must not be reported as successful cleanup. Software running as the same UID can replace files it owns; digest namespaces are not a security boundary.
+
+## NDJSON control version 2
+
+Data frames remain UTF-8 NDJSON with integer `version: 1`; stdout contains data/control frames, stderr only safe diagnostic categories. No unsolicited capability/control frames are inserted into old streams. The existing `history_timezone` command applies only to the requesting SSH subscription, never to an activity lease.
+
+A `capabilities` request contains `version`, `type`, and `requestId`. Its response echoes the ID and returns:
+
+- `capabilities` includes `live_activity_v2`, `live_activity_edges_v1` and `shared_collector_v1` (not `live_activity_v1`); clients requiring immediate activity edges must require `live_activity_edges_v1`;
+- `controlVersion: 2`;
+- `subscribeTimeoutMs: 75000`;
+- `minPublishIntervalMs: 10000`;
+- `relayUrl: "https://agent-speed.yooztech.com"`.
+
+`live_activity_subscribe` retains `requestId`, `relayUrl`, `lease`, `activityId`, `leaseId`, and absolute integer Unix-millisecond `expiresAt`. It requires the following additional fields:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `controlVersion` | Integer 2; clients must negotiate `live_activity_v2`. |
+| `hostId`, `hostName` | Exact strings used by the phone to register the activity with the relay. These are not the collector machine ID or a guessed hostname. |
+| `timezone` | Valid named IANA zone, fixed for this lease. A different activity timezone requires rotation and a new subscription. |
+
+`expiresAt` must be in the future and no more than eight hours away. The bearer is opaque; the collector neither decodes nor extends it. Request and lease identifiers, waiter count, active leases and replay state are bounded.
+
+The response type remains `live_activity_subscribe_ack`. Both positive and negative responses contain integer `version: 1`, `controlVersion: 2`, the exact `requestId`, `activityId`, `leaseId`, `expiresAt`, and Boolean `accepted`. Negative responses additionally contain a safe `error`: `invalid_request`, `conflicting_request`, `busy`, `timeout`, `cancelled`, or `relay_rejected`. A positive response is emitted only after an HTTP 2xx first publish of a fresh real collector sample; parsing a control frame or storing a lease is not confirmation.
+
+The phone must retain a 5-second control-write timeout but wait **90 seconds** for a subscribe response. Capability and unsubscribe requests may keep their shorter wait. A controller timeout, foreground cancellation or stale generation is not proof that the server never received the request: send an unsubscribe for that exact lease and independently revoke the relay activity; persist failed revocation work. Late acknowledgments must not restore display authority. A candidate whose retry deadline cannot fit within 75 seconds fails rather than acknowledging early, bypassing relay limits, or reusing an old sample.
+
+Repeated requests for the same activity/lease and identical binding join the same publisher and first-success outcome, including across SSH reconnects. Request-ID correlation is scoped to the requesting SSH client. Reusing a client's request ID with a changed binding fails. Conflicting host/name/zone/token/expiry under one lease fails. A new lease replaces the old lease for that activity; retired lease IDs are fenced so a delayed retry cannot displace its replacement. In-flight responses are generation-fenced. The relay independently serializes management and publishing; it is the authority over whether old bearer material is valid.
+
+`live_activity_unsubscribe` requires integer `controlVersion: 2`, `requestId`, and `leaseId` in addition to the v1 envelope. `live_activity_unsubscribe_ack` echoes request/lease IDs, `controlVersion: 2`, and `accepted`. Removing an absent lease is idempotent. It removes only that lease, not a newer activity replacement. SSH disconnect does not revoke a confirmed lease.
+
+The only accepted relay URL spellings are the exact HTTPS origin above, optionally with one trailing slash; both map to its fixed `/v1/publish` endpoint. Ports, credentials, case aliases, path/query/fragment additions and other origins are refused. This is not a general-purpose HTTP requester.
+
+## Publishing and failures
+
+Publishing runs on a dedicated worker thread; socket clients and the collection thread do not perform network I/O. There is one latest sample per lease, not a retry backlog. Sampling remains approximately once per second; after a successful response, ordinary attempts wait at least ten seconds from response completion, so network latency cannot shorten the relay's arrival-based window. A known idle-to-active or active-to-idle transition may send the latest sample immediately, bypassing only that routine interval. A positive `generating` or `tools` count means active; both zero mean idle; negative or otherwise unknown counts do not imply idle. Speed is not used. The first known state establishes a baseline; unknown samples do not change it. The state is consumed when an attempt starts, not when it succeeds, and transitions during a request or backoff collapse to the latest state. Failure backoff, `Retry-After`, freshness and lease limits still apply. No priority field is added: the relay determines priority itself. The body contains only `snapshot` with the Worker-supported minimal fields:
+
+`version`, `hostId`, `hostName`, `sampledAt`, `status`, `speed`, `generating`, `tools`, `todayOutput`, `rxBytesPerSecond`, `txBytesPerSecond`, `title`.
+
+`sampledAt` is the sampled snapshot's real `nowMs`; status is `live`; speed is the complete live-output rate or null; daily output uses the fixed activity timezone; traffic is the physical machine receive/transmit rate or null; title is null. Raw logs, project paths, commands, histories and SSH credentials are not uploaded. A dead or backward-clock sampler cannot be made fresh by changing send time. Samples must be newer than the last attempted sample, at most 30 seconds old and not in the future locally. The relay additionally enforces its own freshness/strict-order checks.
+
+The in-process libcurl transport verifies TLS certificates and hostnames, permits HTTPS only, follows no redirects, ignores proxy and netrc environment configuration, limits connection/DNS to three seconds and the entire request to six seconds, and bounds response bodies to 4096 bytes. Asynchronous DNS support is required. A failed transport produces no raw provider error/body/authorization logging. Bearers are never passed in argv, environment variables or URLs.
+
+Before receiving controls, production bridges disable core dumps and set `PR_SET_DUMPABLE=0`; the daemon inherits those restrictions. Unexpected exceptions produce only a generic safe error. The fixed-loopback TLS smoke build omits only the non-dumpable flag so its disposable processes can be traced; it still disables core dumps and must never receive real credentials or be distributed.
+
+- 400: discard that sample; only a fresh sample may follow. Eight consecutive invalid snapshots retire the authorization rather than sending a known-bad stream forever.
+- 401/403/410, and oversized-body 413: stop and retire the lease. The collector does not obtain replacement authority.
+- 429: honor `Retry-After`, at least ten seconds; `apns_backoff` requires at least sixty seconds. Confirmation and lease expiry still apply.
+- 502/transport failures: bounded exponential backoff, latest fresh sample only. Confirmed authorization remains bounded by expiry and can recover after an outage.
+
+The relay reserves `lastSentAt` and `lastSample` before APNs I/O. Thus even a 502 cannot be retried with the same sample. Rotation preserves the relay's rate window; a newly rotated lease may legitimately wait longer than the old five-second mobile timeout.
+
+## Build and verification surfaces
+
+Build the prepared tree with CMake 3.22+, C++20, pkg-config, zstd, Brotli, SQLite, ICU, OpenSSL and libcurl development libraries. On Ubuntu add `libcurl4-openssl-dev`; runtime requires matching libcurl/OpenSSL/ICU and other linked libraries plus CA certificates and timezone data. Qt, sudo and an external curl subprocess are not needed by the collector. Use `macos/scripts/build-linux-collector.sh` for native-architecture-only artifacts and `SHA256SUMS`; do not label x86_64 bytes as ARM64.
+
+CMake's normal test executable uses a separately compiled test-only HOME namespace and one-second idle period so process tests cannot attach to the maintainer's canonical daemon. Neither override exists in the distribution executable. `AGENTSPEED_BUILD_SSH_SMOKE=ON` additionally builds the explicitly non-distributable `agentspeed-collector-ssh-smoke`: it uses the same libcurl path but a compile-time fixed localhost TLS endpoint and test CA, with canonical passwd-home and production 30-second idle behavior. Never package that executable.
+
+`linux/tests/shared_collector_ssh_smoke.py` runs a local sshd, a disposable Unix account and an isolated TLS relay, then cleans them up. It requires local root only for that isolated account/sshd; it never accesses a production host or modifies a system SSH service. Its JSON results identify actual scenarios and limits. It does not prove Cloudflare deployment, APNs acceptance or iPhone delivery. Real signed-iPhone testing, deployed Worker/App Attest configuration, Apple credentials, ARM64 runtime, and native Mac/Windows UI remain separate acceptance prerequisites.
